---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/deliverability";
const title = "Shopify email deliverability: diagnose before increasing volume";
const desc = "Inspect authentication, recipient-level delivery responses and audience changes with a documented pause and recovery process.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does authentication guarantee inbox placement?",
    "answer": "No. It establishes technical identity checks, while receivers also evaluate traffic and recipient feedback. Verify the actual message and investigate receiver responses."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every new sender use the same warmup schedule?",
    "answer": "No. Plan a staged rollout with the provider using infrastructure history, permitted audience and observed feedback. Hold expansion when the evidence worsens."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do seed accounts prove customer delivery?",
    "answer": "They provide limited observations for those accounts. Combine them with receiver responses, available provider data and customer reports, without generalizing to the whole list."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">Operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Define the failure precisely</h2>
      <p>A message accepted by a receiving server is not proof it reached the inbox or was read. Separate acceptance, temporary deferral, permanent rejection, spam placement, complaints and conversion. Capture the receiving domain, sending stream, timestamp, response code, campaign and infrastructure change before choosing a remedy.</p>
      <p>Compare affected traffic with a stable cohort using the same time window and denominator. An aggregate open-rate drop can reflect audience mix or privacy-related measurement changes. Do not infer a DNS failure from opens alone, or describe the Promotions category as a delivery failure. A seed test offers a limited observation about its test accounts, not the whole customer audience.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Read the current receiver requirements</h2>
      <p>Google’s sender guidance applies to personal Gmail accounts. It requires SPF or DKIM for all senders, and SPF, DKIM and DMARC for bulk senders in its stated daily-volume category. Bulk direct mail must align the From domain with SPF or DKIM; marketing and subscribed messages need one-click unsubscribe and a visible body link. The guidance also covers TLS, DNS, formatting and spam rates.</p>
      <p>Use the linked live guidance to determine the applicable category and implementation details. Other receivers maintain their own policies; Gmail compliance is not a universal receiver certificate. Meeting technical requirements does not guarantee inbox placement. Assign an owner to recheck receiver rules and the actual configuration after a sending change.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Audit the message that actually left the platform</h2>
      <p>Collect headers from a controlled message on each active stream. Inspect Authentication-Results, the signing domain, envelope sender and visible From address. Compare them with the platform’s required DNS records. A green dashboard for one domain does not establish alignment for a different From address or a forgotten secondary sender.</p>
      <p>List every authorized sender before changing DNS or strengthening DMARC policy. Include support, order notifications, invoices and marketing. Give the DNS owner the exact proposed change and rollback record; do not paste a second SPF record over an existing setup. Ask the provider which infrastructure settings it manages and which belong to the merchant.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Separate domain decisions from IP decisions</h2>
      <p>A branded sending domain, a marketing subdomain and a dedicated IP solve different configuration problems. Ask the provider to explain the actual signing and envelope domains, shared or dedicated IP arrangement, monitoring access and volume expectations. Do not buy a dedicated IP solely because the list reached an arbitrary size.</p>
      <p>Splitting streams can help operations identify failures and manage policy, but it does not create guaranteed reputation isolation or reset receiver trust. Keep necessary service messages stable while investigating promotions. Moving everything to a new domain during an incident can introduce another unknown rather than repair the underlying audience problem.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Investigate audience changes before mailing more people</h2>
      <p>Reconcile recent imports, giveaway sources, new popups and dormant segments with the send log. Check permission and suppression records before considering engagement. An address-validation tool cannot prove consent or that a person wants the campaign. Do not mail a purchased or unexplained list to discover whether it is usable.</p>
      <p>Define active audiences with multiple appropriate signals, including reliable clicks and customer actions, while recognizing that automated clicks also exist. Use the buying cycle and permission history to set review windows. A fixed non-open duration is not a universal retirement rule. Keep uncertain cohorts on hold rather than automatically sending a re-permission message.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Make unsubscribe and bounce handling observable</h2>
      <p>Inspect both the visible body opt-out and the provider-generated unsubscribe headers on the delivered message. Rehearse the action using controlled addresses and verify durable suppression, downstream propagation and queued-send removal. Editing a footer link alone does not establish one-click header behavior.</p>
      <p>Keep permanent address failures suppressed according to the provider’s handling rules and investigate recurring temporary failures by receiver response. A temporary deferral is not permission to retry aggressively through a second tool. Preserve complaint and withdrawal stops during imports, and ask how the provider handles feedback that is unavailable from a particular receiver.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Ramp against feedback, not a universal calendar</h2>
      <p>Before increasing volume, establish a stable permitted cohort and record normal traffic by receiver and stream. Agree with the provider on a staged plan appropriate to infrastructure history and the campaign schedule. At each stage inspect deferrals, rejection reasons, complaint signals and support reports before expanding.</p>
      <p>Hold or reduce discretionary traffic when feedback worsens; correct the identified cause before resuming. Record the decision and the evidence required for the next step. A fixed fourteen-day schedule cannot promise a warmed domain, and passing a small staff send cannot establish performance at production scale. Do not use necessary transactional messages as artificial warmup traffic.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Respond to an incident with a bounded change</h2>
      <p>If one receiver starts deferring a campaign, pause further expansion to the affected traffic and collect exact responses. Compare the last successful send, recent DNS changes, content, source mix and volume. Open a provider case with redacted message identifiers and timing; do not repeatedly resend the whole list while waiting.</p>
      <p>Name the person who can pause each marketing sender, the technical contact and the support owner. Maintain necessary service delivery through its established route. When an issue is corrected, resume a controlled permitted cohort with monitoring. There is no promised recovery period or guaranteed revenue rebound; keep a dated incident record with observed outcomes only.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Prepare peak sends and migrations using the same evidence</h2>
      <p>Before a major promotion, review the planned audience and frequency against normal receiver traffic. Reconcile exclusions across campaigns and lifecycle messages. Confirm staff coverage, provider escalation and a way to stop queued batches. Choose a change window the team can support rather than banning entire calendar months.</p>
      <p>For migration, preserve suppression and consent records, document authentication responsibilities and assign one live sender per journey. Validate new headers before ramping. Keep historical logs accessible and a rollback that restores ownership without replaying stale queues. Do not run two live cart journeys as a deliverability comparison.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Select tools by diagnostic access and total workload</h2>
      <p>Ask Sequenzy and any other candidate to show a rejected message, a deferred message, complaint handling, domain setup and exportable suppression evidence in the proposed plan. Ask who investigates receiver-specific failures and whether message-level logs expire. A product’s reputation claim does not replace evidence from the merchant’s traffic.</p>
      <p>Budget monitoring, operator time, provider support and permitted send volume alongside platform fees. Review delivery feedback by receiver and cohort with business outcomes after refunds, without converting attributed revenue or open-rate changes into claimed incremental gains. Handoff should include the current configuration, recent changes, incident owner, pause control and next evidence review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Primary sender reference</h2><p>Reviewed 25 November 2026: <a class="link" href="https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126?hl=en">Google email sender guidelines</a>. Apply its personal Gmail scope and consult the live requirements before changing production traffic.</p></div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related guidance">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">App evaluations</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">Consent</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Discount discipline</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Migration</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
